,"status":"discovered","validation_status":"pending"},"changed_fields":["progress.status","progress.extraction_status","progress.paper_card_status","progress.branch_update_status","progress.validation_status","paper-card","language-units","model-units","learning-audit","observations","extraction.visual_review","type-branch.A.rules","type-branch.A.rule-impact-report"],"event_hash":"b31b185be5de658274f14903b36dcec2356fa5d5a16142359377c85de9284ff2","event_id":"TX-000096-9fbf56fe5c9b","event_type":"paper-closed","history_completeness":"native","paper_id":"P-A-2019-1917933-483a8f92","previous_event_hash":"ee503655575a0f89d5f4799f4366d0fea40e755914c2ec1854135f880eb6e224","sequence":96,"timestamp_utc":"2026-07-15T16:35:30Z","type_branch_diff":{"new_language_unit_count":225,"new_model_count":12,"new_observation_count":418,"new_rule_impact_count":15,"problem_type":"A","section_contracts_after":17,"section_contracts_before":0,"shared_catalog_changes":[]},"unresolved_added":["The official problem states a roughly 10 kg hatch mass and asks teams to assume that basic biology is accurate, but the paper replaces it with 5 kg because the authors prefer a bat analogy.","The energy model uses BMR plus human activity multipliers and never separately accounts for long-distance flight, fire breathing, trauma resistance, hunting mechanics, thermoregulation, or reproduction.","Balerion is first described as roughly 42 m long and then scaled as 50 m long from crocodile teeth without reconciling which length drives mass.","Scaling a 250 kg, 9 m-wingspan pterosaur to 150 m by the paper's cubic law gives about 1,157,407 kg, not 115,740 kg; the printed estimate is low by a factor of ten.","Correcting the pterosaur endpoint makes the mean of the pterosaur and Komodo estimates about 740,741 kg rather than 219,901 kg, changing every downstream upper-state result.","The model-selection prose says dragons grow without limit and therefore should not have an asymptote, but it selects a von Bertalanffy model with finite L_inf and W_inf.","The condition d^2y/dx^2 < 1 does not define attenuating unbounded growth; concavity would require a sign comparison with zero and still would not guarantee an unbounded function.","Citation [24] is used to support von Bertalanffy or indeterminate growth, but reference [24] is a Smithsonian Komodo-dragon page; the indeterminate-growth item is [25].","The episode table assigns 2.5 years to both 3 ft and 7 ft and 3 years to both 12 ft and 25 ft; the paper says points were adjusted but never publishes the adjusted dataset or fit ledger.","The final growth equation mixes x inside an equation defined as W(t), leaving the age symbol inconsistent across the model and later evaluations.","t0 is described as the t value such that W(t)=5, but in the equation it is a shift parameter; t0=1.203 makes W(0) about 5 rather than W(1.203)=5.","After deriving B=65.3M^-0.25 from the 100 kg reference, the age-composed line prints B(t)=20.65W(t)^-0.25 and silently drops the reference-mass factor.","The stated 0.3-9 W/kg range converts to about 6.20-185.85 kcal/(kg day), not 3-92; the conversion is approximately one-half of the correct range.","At 219,901 kg and 500,000 kcal per cow, the corrected broad rate range is about 2.72-81.74 cows/day, not 1.32-40.46.","The blue-whale daily-energy statement cites [25], which is an indeterminate-growth source; [26] discusses calories swallowed in one mouthful and does not directly establish daily BMR.","57,728 multiplied by the lightly-active factor 1.375 is about 79,376, not 74,324; the captive activity interval is understated.","The midpoint of the correct sedentary-to-light interval is about 74,325, not 71,799; the erroneous value is reused in the water model.","The calorie-over-time graph plateaus near 2.3e5 kcal/day, while the revised formula C=177.4W^0.75 approaches about 1.82e6 kcal/day at W_inf; the graph appears to use an older coefficient.","The low-mass sensitivity equation is visually printed with a positive exponent, producing large negative mass after cubing rather than the plotted positive saturating curve.","The high-mass sensitivity equation is also visually printed with a positive exponent and cannot generate the displayed positive saturating curve.","The sensitivity plot evidently uses negative exponents while both displayed formulas use positive exponents, so the figure and equation interfaces disagree.","Even restoring negative signs to the rounded low/high equations yields age-220 masses around 117,102 and 313,702 kg, not exactly the asserted 115,740 and 324,074 kg endpoints.","The proposed food/space extension only lowers W_inf; without refitting K and t0 this also changes birth and one-year mass rather than isolating an adult resource limit.","The land model reverts to C=65.3W^0.75 after the paper supersedes it with C=177.4W^0.75, understating every land result by about 2.7167.","Using the revised coefficient and the paper's same biome densities gives about 5,793 square miles for three dragons in temperate forest and 57,933 in arctic tundra, not 2,132.4 and 21,324.7.","N is described and tabled as calories per square mile, but because food intake is daily its unit is kcal/(day square mile); the missing time unit obscures dimensional closure.","W denotes dragon mass in the growth and metabolic models but denotes predator food weight in the land equation, creating a cross-model symbol collision.","The paper cites [6] for a universal 1490 kcal/lb meat conversion, but [6] concerns metabolic scaling rather than meat energy density.","Wolf and likely lion rows use group-level food and territory while other rows appear individual; the entity level is not defined consistently across biomes.","The model treats dragon intake as replacing a natural apex predator rather than adding a new consumer; the authors acknowledge this only after reporting area results.","Ecological impact is not quantitatively modeled beyond proxy land: fire, species loss, competition, waste, water depletion, methane, and incumbent predator effects remain qualitative.","The weighted metabolic-water yield is 0.0508 g/kJ; converting to per kcal requires multiplication by 4.184, giving about 0.21255 g/kcal rather than the paper's 0.086.","Using the paper's 0.689 g/kcal water need and corrected 0.21255 g/kcal metabolic yield gives a multiplier about 3.24 and roughly 18.5 hours, not 7.5 hours.","Dividing kg/day by kcal/day yields g/kcal; the paper retains an extra /day in its normalized water-intensity unit.","0.689 g/kcal equals 0.000689 L/kcal, not 0.00689 L/kcal; the freshwater conversion is high by a factor of ten.","The printed freshwater intensity implies about 494.7 L/day at 71,799 kcal, contradicting the same page's 49.46 kg/day water requirement.","The human is assumed to be 90% water and all 55.8 kg is treated as available, despite no source or body-composition boundary for that value.","One feeding scene is assumed to provide exactly one day of both calories and water with 100% extraction efficiency; adequacy and assimilation are unobserved.","A 2.5-day bird migration is shortened linearly by a water-yield ratio without showing comparable fat/protein reserve mass, dehydration threshold, flight power, or body scaling for dragons.","Water need is calibrated from sedentary/light activity calories and then applied to continuous flight, so the activity state changes between calibration and use.","57,728 multiplied by 1.2 is 69,273.6, not the printed 69,264 kcal/day.","The paragraph says hot conditions induce rest and cold conditions induce more activity, but its final sentence says calorie demand grows with larger temperature values; Summary and letter say cold demand is higher.","The claimed temperature logistic model has no equation, temperature unit, direction, midpoint, slope, data, figure, or fitting rule; two horizontal bounds do not identify it.","Neither the water nor temperature correction is propagated back into biome land, cow demand, farm area, or community labor, so the climate branch does not close the resource chain.","The paper's own timings imply a repeat calving interval near 283+55=338 days; adding the 730-day maturation lag to obtain 1068 breeder cows confuses throughput cycle with pipeline delay.","The 730-day maturation delay is included once in the 1068x breeder count and again as 730x immature cows, double-counting the same delay.","The cow pipeline does not explicitly close sex ratio, failed pregnancies, calf mortality, nonbreeding age, replacement timing, or whether every carcass remains nutritionally usable.","All 5394x cattle, including immature cohorts, are assigned a 2,000 lb adult weight and 4% daily forage intake, inconsistent with the paper's own age structure.","x is never evaluated from C(t)/500000 for a named dragon age and activity state, so actual herd, acre, and people totals are never produced.","Summary defines x as the amount of people per day, while section 6.3 defines x as cows required per dragon per day.","61x+48 includes only direct farm workers, three riders, and average security; it is not a total community size and omits households, logistics, machinery, veterinary care, and services.","The claim that high body temperature prevents any disease, analogized to bats, is uncited and biologically absolute.","The reproduction section says prior models can find carrying capacity but never computes it or supplies egg production, hatching, mortality, or a population trajectory.","The inference that dragons can mate regardless of genetic relation because humans in the story accept incest does not provide biological evidence about dragon reproduction.","The letter calls Rhaegal 'Rhaegar,' changing the named dragon.","The letter says dragons grow indefinitely, while the body explicitly uses a finite W_inf von Bertalanffy curve.","The letter presents 3.6 cows/day as the largest-dragon food limit, but 3.6 is basal demand; the paper's own multipliers imply at least 4.32 sedentary and about 6.21 very-active cows/day.","The assumptions say dragons need red meat and red meat alone, immediately say they can also eat fish, and the letter changes the claim to meat and fish without reconciling nutrition.","The paper classifies dragons as endotherms but later justifies temperature behavior using reptilian behavioral thermoregulation without explaining how the mechanisms combine.","The required letter discusses arid and cold/arctic conditions but gives almost no warm-temperate comparison or quantitative migration resource change.","The official varying-assistance requirement is reduced to one 100% human-provision scenario; partial hunting is mentioned only qualitatively and no assistance parameter or scenario table is supplied.","Three-dragon land is computed as three copies of Balerion rather than using the ages and masses of the three living dragons or showing land growth over time.","Summary reports no land-area number, no evaluated community population, and no final growth formula; its only community expression also defines x incorrectly.","The three growth sensitivity curves have no legend, so low, central, and high cases cannot be identified from the figure alone.","The paper's analytical figures and tables have no Figure/Table numbers or formal captions; the growth input table also lacks source-to-row labeling.","The episode size table is not connected to a transparent retained-versus-adjusted flag, so a reader cannot reproduce which rows informed the final curve.","The body uses the superseded 65.3 coefficient for land while the letter uses 3.6 cows/day from the revised 177.4 coefficient, so the stakeholder outputs mix incompatible model versions.","No code, parameter solver, residuals, holdout comparison, or external dragon analogue validation is provided for the growth curve.","Several quantitative inputs rely on general web pages, a calculator, Yahoo Answers, and species summaries without a source-quality hierarchy or retained data ledger.","Sensitivity is primarily endpoint substitution and prose; no joint uncertainty, probability model, interactions, confidence intervals, or recommendation-reversal test is performed."],"unresolved_resolved":[],"validation_results":[{"details":"All 23 physical pages passed text, rendered-pixel, and complete visual-review checks; all page texts, three contact sheets, and original-resolution formula or visual pages 7, 9, 10, 12, 15, 16, 18, and 19 were inspected.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_extraction.py"},{"details":"Two hundred twenty-five positive units implement 75 source-grounded action classes across 17 quantitative layouts; saturation was 60, 15, then zero new actions.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_language_units.py"},{"details":"Twelve model units, 14 dependency edges, and 29 retained or reconstructed formula interfaces preserve the mass, growth, metabolism, land, water, climate, cow-pipeline, community, reproduction, and sensitivity chains.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_model_units.py"},{"details":"The language-first audit passed with 17 five-part contracts, 10 main argument chains, exact model and visual retention, 225 positive dispositions, and 70 warnings isolated to G7.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_learning_audit.py"},{"details":"Paper card, 418 observations, 9 important visuals, complete extraction, language, models, audit, and 15 live A-rule impacts cross-validated against 41 A rules.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_paper_artifacts.py"},{"details":"Fifteen declared A-rule impacts appended nominated evidence, preserved rule lifecycle, and produced staged/live byte-identical rules and impact-report files.","status":"pass","validator":"apply_rule_impacts.py"},{"details":"All 114 regression tests passed against the final live bytes.","status":"pass","validator":"python -m unittest discover -s tests -v"},{"details":"The authoritative G0-G8 language-first order and prospective/live transaction protocol re