us":"discovered","validation_status":"pending"},"changed_fields":["progress.status","progress.extraction_status","progress.paper_card_status","progress.branch_update_status","progress.validation_status","paper-card","language-units","model-units","learning-audit","observations","extraction.visual_review","type-branch.A.rules","type-branch.A.rule-impact-report"],"event_hash":"4f785ef169867a6b2330a2736532b4652f60b3d2f91e47159ee12adcba01096d","event_id":"TX-000098-b721b820360b","event_type":"paper-closed","history_completeness":"native","paper_id":"P-A-2019-1924781-9e0bc03c","previous_event_hash":"799a284e98e15b3eab92301850751d10086e0ca770a81670109f68261ea9ecea","sequence":98,"timestamp_utc":"2026-07-15T18:07:58Z","type_branch_diff":{"new_language_unit_count":285,"new_model_count":12,"new_observation_count":472,"new_rule_impact_count":11,"problem_type":"A","section_contracts_after":15,"section_contracts_before":0,"shared_catalog_changes":[]},"unresolved_added":["The Summary reports 570 km2 as the warm-temperate requirement, but the body says all three dragons eventually die at 570 km2 and survive at 600 km2.","The Summary says the warm-temperate dragons grow to 4,000,000 kg without saying collective or per-dragon, while the body reports a three-dragon collective equilibrium near 4.4 million kg.","The Summary reports an arctic requirement of 42,000 km2 and mass of 3,500,000 kg without a traceable body simulation for that pair.","The Summary calls 110,000 km2 the hot-arid requirement and 86,000 kg each, but the displayed 80,000 km2 body run supports only two dragons at about 200,000 kg combined.","The Summary gives no community-size result and no comparison across assistance levels, a central official requirement.","The four-part decomposition omits the required community-size-under-varying-assistance task rather than routing it to a model section.","The logic framework names the major mathematical modules but does not show temperature, water, flight, fire, trauma, community assistance, or migration-energy pathways.","The paper assumes television continuity overrides conflicting book material without itemizing which source facts change or how uncertainty is handled.","The paper invokes magic for physical discrepancies and later presents a physical infeasibility audit, but it never defines which claims remain subject to real physics.","The assumption that dragons grow indefinitely conflicts with the finite logistic asymptote used as the unrestricted growth curve.","Uniform ages are assumed without a lifespan, sex ratio, maturity age, or reproduction model that could support an age distribution.","The no-taste-preference assumption reduces hunting to relative mass and density, omitting habitat, defense, accessibility, season, and prey behavior.","Drogon is treated as a maximum-resource growth case from narrative access to livestock, but no intake history or uncertainty supports an upper-envelope calibration.","Dragon-dragon, prey-prey, and broader food-web interactions are excluded even though the paper later claims ecosystem stability.","Countrywide prey populations are assumed to be at steady equilibrium before dragon entry, but static counts do not identify net growth equilibrium.","The fixed 60 percent peak-mass death trigger is borrowed from a human analogy and is not calibrated to dragon age, fat reserve, or starvation duration.","The discontinuous starvation threshold controls near-area results but is not included in sensitivity analysis.","The 90 percent food efficiency and universal 2500 kcal/kg meat value are asserted without prey composition, assimilation, waste, or uncertainty evidence.","Mixed height, wingspan, and mass observations are combined without an explicit conversion ledger or covariance model.","The television-derived dimensions and their standard deviations do not document frame selection, perspective correction, annotator variability, or source images.","The Balerion illustration is treated as a scale drawing even though perspective, artistic stylization, and Aegon's pose are not propagated into uncertainty.","The Balerion cubic transfer reports roughly 6,000,000 kg but does not retain the exact Drogon mass and linear dimension bundle needed to reproduce it.","The logistic fit reports L=843.717523 and k=0.71173635 but omits t0, so Equation 1 cannot be evaluated from the paper.","The claimed chi-square fit has no statistic, degrees of freedom, p-value, residual table, fitted-point list, or complete parameter vector.","The paper describes Equation 2 as a function of current mass but does not show the height-to-mass coefficient a or the units of L in that interface.","The day conversion in Equation 2 is fixed at 365 while the logistic age data and parameter units are not explicitly reconciled.","Equation 3 and its coefficient line do not state the units of M or B, despite log transforms requiring a defined reference scale.","The dragon body temperature T used in the curved allometric equation is never given.","At T=310 K the printed base-10 equation gives about 2,002 W, while the same coefficients interpreted with natural logs give about 585,411 W; the letter's 5,392 kg/day food value closely matches the natural-log route, not the printed log10 route.","The allometric regression is extrapolated far beyond observed animals without prediction intervals, alternate exponents, or sensitivity to body temperature.","The piecewise preference density is mathematically normalized by A=0.8976, but the paper does not show the integral or clarify that its support extends beyond the plotted x=3.5 range.","The prose suggests prey up to roughly three times dragon mass, but the right Gaussian has no hard cutoff and retains positive probability beyond x=3.","The two Gaussian widths and their asymmetry are chosen narratively rather than estimated from observed hunting behavior.","The preference density is not empirically calibrated to hunting observations and does not model the higher absolute cost of attacking very large prey.","The scarcity factor raises a dimensioned prey-density quantity to a noninteger power without a reference density, making alpha unit-dependent.","Scarcity parameters were selected from runs whose behavior looked expected and then fit by a power law, a circular calibration procedure the authors acknowledge.","No objective loss, labeled calibration data, holdout scenario, or confidence interval supports alpha=13.2948 and gamma=-0.425.","The Lotka-Volterra scaffold uses x and y as population states but the final system replaces the predator population with separate dragon masses without a formal derivation.","Prey natural growth is reduced to births minus deaths without sex ratio, maturity, gestation interval, density dependence, or seasonal timing.","Equation 7 adds m_D'/0.9 to 2500B even though the first term is kg/day and, with B in kcal/day and 2500 kcal/kg, the second printed product is not kg/day.","Converting BMR from kcal/day to food mass requires division by 2500 kcal/kg, not multiplication; Equation 7 has the conversion direction reversed.","Equation 8 again subtracts 2500B from consumed prey mass, so its two sides are dimensionally incompatible as printed.","Even ignoring the BMR unit problem, abundant prey makes Equations 7-8 reduce to dm_D/dt=m_D'/0.9, exceeding the declared unrestricted maximum gain by 11.1 percent.","The plotted dragon state is collective mass, while the preference ratio and 60 percent death event require per-dragon masses and peak histories; the aggregate-to-individual interface is undefined.","The temperate collective peak near 6.5 million kg is close to the 6.813 million kg single-dragon logistic asymptote, suggesting the code may apply a single-dragon cap to an aggregate state.","The letter's 5,392 kg/day food figure cannot be reproduced from the printed multiplication and therefore appears to come from a different implementation.","Equations 7-8 use basal metabolism only and omit flight, hunting, fire breathing, combat, thermoregulation, reproduction, and injury recovery.","The preference allocation denominator and initial/current prey-mass ratio have no defined behavior when a preferred species or total prey mass approaches zero.","Equation 9 treats fractional eaten mass divided by individual mass as continuous deaths, without nonnegativity events or integer handling.","The ODE solver is described only as a Python wrapper switching Adams and BDF; solver name, tolerances, Jacobian, event handling, and source code are absent.","Country-to-region population scaling is described in prose but no equation, country area, or row-by-row local initial state is published.","The New Zealand table mixes domestic livestock with wild species without modeling continued human breeding, husbandry, slaughter, imports, or land limits.","The 600 km2 run is described as a steady state without a numerical convergence criterion or horizon-sensitivity check.","The temperate body result labels 4.4 million kg as the combined mass of all three dragons, unlike the Summary and Conclusion's ambiguous entity level.","At 570 km2 one dragon dies and the remaining two later starve, so the run is evidence of insufficiency rather than the minimum supporting area claimed elsewhere.","The arid input populations are explicitly highly estimated, yet the final area and mass claims carry no uncertainty range.","The 80,000 km2 arid run ends with only two dragons; it does not directly validate the three-dragon 110,000 km2 headline.","The arid outcome is dominated by one small, guessed Barbary sheep population, making the threshold highly sensitive to an acknowledged low-quality input.","At 27,300 km2 the arctic body run loses one dragon around day 4,800 and ends with two dragons near 270,000 kg combined.","The arctic prose calls the remaining community completely stable despite continued caribou decline and without an equilibrium test.","The 400 km2 death-off experiment changes the model class by removing mortality and should be treated as a diagnostic, not survival validation.","The paper admits additional simulation is needed to know whether moose eventually go extinct, so the plotted finite-horizon behavior cannot establish long-run stability.","The generalized invasive-predator application removes discrete dragon deaths but does not add predator reproduction, mortality, age, or calibration for a real species.","The stinkbug example is an invasive prey/pest rather than an invasive apex predator and does not match the model's trophic mechanism.","The sensitivity section varies only area; it omits mortality threshold, prey data, preference, scarcity, metabolism, initial masses, and solver settings.","The refined sensitivity text claims 27,100 km2 sustains all three dragons, conflicting with the displayed 27,300 km2 run where one dragon dies.","The sensitivity mosaics do not label every panel in an ordered outcome table, state a shared horizon, or quantify the survival decision rule.","The authors acknowledge estimates and guesses in IUCN-derived population data but do not identify each guessed row or propagate the uncertainty.","The authors acknowledge omitted dragon-dragon and prey-prey interactions, which invalidates broad claims about a stable ecosystem rather than only refining them.","The self-critique of scarcity-factor manipulation gives no replacement calibration strategy or evidence collection plan.","The leg-pressure audit uses a 10^4 kg adult example, inconsistent with the million-kilogram masses used in the growth and climate results.","The wing-loading audit likewise uses 10^4 kg and cites an unresolved [?] reference, so its 500 m2 requirement is neither source-supported nor aligned with the modeled dragon.","The thermodynamic mortality argument is qualitative and does not estimate lifespan, senescence, or damage repair.","The claim that three-quarter-power maintenance demand outpaces energy supply is logically unsupported: sublinear demand alone does not exceed linearly scaling supply.","Figure 3 has no axis variables, units, functional forms, data, or fitted intersection and cannot validate the claimed energy limit.","The Conclusion reports 570 km2 as the temperate requirement despite the body showing total dragon extinction at that area.","The Conclusion reports 42,000 km2 and 3,500,000 kg for the arctic case without reconciling 27,100 and 27,300 km2 or the body endpoint near 270,000 kg.","The Conclusion reports 110,000 km2 and 86,000 kg each for the arid case without presenting the supporting three-dragon trajectory or threshold search.","The letter applies a 3,444,574 kg season-7 Drogon state to body simulations that start with three newly hatched dragons, without a matching transient rerun.","The letter's 5,392 kg/day food estimate lacks an activity multiplier, flight, combat, fire, hunting, and cold-temperature expenditure.","The letter says one temperate dragon can feed indefinitely, but the displayed temperate simulations concern three hatchlings and do not validate current-mass Drogon.","The letter warns Drogon cannot remain in the North for more than a few days, but no migration time, travel distance, flight cost, battle cost, or finite-duration simulation supports that limit.","The two-page letter gives no quantitative table comparing warm temperate, hot arid, and arctic requirements under consistent entity and time bases.","No section computes required community population, labor, supply chain, land, or infrastructure under any assistance level.","Only eight references support narrative facts, the ODE scaffold, population data, allometry, diet, and scaling; many table entries and physical claims lack row-level citations.","The unresolved [?] wing-loading citation and sparse web-source metadata prevent reliable source tracing."],"unresolved_resolved":[],"validation_results":[{"details":"All 22 rendered pages passed text, nonblank-pixel, and complete visual-review checks; all page texts, three contact sheets, three detail sheets, and original-resolution formula/result pages were inspected.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_extraction.py"},{"details":"285 positive units implement 95 source-grounded language action classes across 15 quantitative chapter layouts; Abstract and Introduction preserve paragraph chains and the third saturation pass added zero reusable actions.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_language_units.py"},{"details":"12 connected model units, 14 dependency edges, and 28 visually checked interfaces retain the growth, allometry, preference, scarcity, ODE, death, climate-scaling, and threshold chains.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_model_units.py"},{"details":"The language-first audit passed with 15 five-part contracts, exact model/equation and visual retention, 285 positive dispositions, and 86 warnings isolated to G7.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_learning_audit.py"},{"details":"Paper card, 472 observations, 16 important visuals, complete extraction, language, models, audit, and 11 staged/live A-rule impacts cross-validated against 41 A rules.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_paper_artifacts.py"},{"details":"Eleven declared A-rule impacts appended only nominated evidence, preserved lifecycle, and produced staged/live byte-identical rules and impact-report files.","status":"pass","validator":"apply_rule_impacts.py"},{"details":"All 114 regression tests passed against the final workflow.","status":"pass","validator":"python -m unittest discover -s tests -v"},{"details":"The authoritative G0-G8 language-first order and prospective/live transaction protocol remain valid.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_plan_protocol.py"}]}
