,"changed_fields":["progress.status","progress.extraction_status","progress.paper_card_status","progress.branch_update_status","progress.validation_status","paper-card","language-units","model-units","learning-audit","observations","extraction.visual_review","type-branch.B.rules","type-branch.B.rule-impact-report"],"event_hash":"bbcff6cb87f8d6afd4207d56bcffe427f6dba5bbb2ee3aaeb92479759b0e9a90","event_id":"TX-000150-ea33242d0736","event_type":"paper-closed","history_completeness":"native","paper_id":"P-B-2011-9440-95f0fa40","previous_event_hash":"52a89aeec9f829cc329bd2f8c0f4014694acbe67320d3518e1c6e7a8039cecd2","sequence":150,"timestamp_utc":"2026-07-20T02:42:58Z","type_branch_diff":{"new_equation_count":26,"new_language_unit_count":402,"new_model_count":48,"new_observation_count":1011,"new_rule_impact_count":12,"problem_type":"B","section_contracts_after":17,"section_contracts_before":0,"shared_catalog_changes":[]},"unresolved_added":["The Summary Sheet is completely absent from the text layer even though it contains dense text and two result figures.","The 97.12 percent reachability headline is probabilistic, while the same page admits there is no method guaranteeing universal point-to-point communication.","The Abstract calls eleven repeaters the real optimum without immediately limiting the claim to the idealized continuous circle-covering subproblem.","The Introduction contains an unfinished comparison sentence ending with 'in the later case.'","Different PL tones do not by themselves remove same-frequency or adjacent-frequency radio interference; the paper's predicate is an abstraction.","The frequency and tone counts are regional or problem-specific resources and cannot be generalized as a universal amateur-radio plan.","Only user-to-repeater and repeater-to-repeater directed edges are explicit; bidirectional user communication needs an additional symmetry or downlink assumption.","Assigning every user to the nearest repeater can create avoidable overload when a slightly farther repeater has spare capacity.","The reachable-area definition ignores a user's own direct communication disk and therefore represents a deliberate approximation.","R at least twice Phi is a universal sufficient condition for weak and strong equivalence, not a necessary condition for every particular network.","The free-space model excludes terrain, clutter, fading, antenna patterns, diffraction, atmospheric effects, and receiver implementation losses.","Equation 5 is attributed to Shannon theory although it is the Friis free-space path-loss relation.","Equation 6 mixes quantities originally introduced in watts with a dB-domain additive budget without an explicit conversion convention.","Equation 7 uses the constant 37.2328, while Equations 5-6 and the listed gains and losses imply a value near 3.72 rather than 37.23.","Using ERP from Table 1 and then adding transmitter antenna gain risks double-counting antenna gain.","The 39 dB VHF antenna gain is physically extraordinary and is not labeled dBi or dBd.","Table 1 contains repeated call signs and an old web source but no sampling date, cleaning rule, or summary statistic.","The one-microwatt receive threshold equals about -30 dBm and is far above common VHF receiver sensitivity; a microvolt-versus-microwatt confusion is plausible.","The reported R=85.45 miles and r=15.28 miles inherit the unresolved Equation 7 arithmetic and link-budget assumptions.","Equation 8 gives information rate in bits per second, but the text incorrectly says it is measured in watt or dB.","P_ur is a received power, but the text incorrectly says it is measured in bps.","The CDMA processing-gain relation is transferred to a narrowband repeater problem without a justified air-interface model.","The paper ignores background noise and other-repeater interference while simultaneously using SNR and E_b/N_0 to derive capacity.","The conversions 39 dB=7966.40 W, 0.4 dB=1.07 W, and 18 dB=63.01 W attach watts to dimensionless ratios.","Ten to the power 0.4/10 is about 1.096, not 1.07.","Calling 18 dB the midpoint of 5-30 dB is a rounded choice; the arithmetic midpoint is 17.5 dB.","The reported capacity near 119 is a model output under disputed gain, interference, and unit assumptions rather than an equipment specification.","The continuous approximation permits fractional users and suppresses local load variability until the later correction.","The 324 and 270 limits answer different questions—frequency separation versus avoidance of inverse-pair feedback—and should not be merged.","The five-per-tone non-interacting maximum is argued informally through a pigeonhole statement rather than a complete combinatorial proof.","The statement that when R is at least Phi any pair may interfere is existential or permissive wording, not a guarantee that every pair lies within R.","The area lower bound of seven is necessary only and ignores overlap and boundary inefficiency.","Equation 16 uses C=118 immediately after Equation 12 reports approximately 119, without explaining the conservative rounding.","Several Table 2 covering radii are described as hand calculations without derivations.","The twelve-cell conclusion excludes eleven only within the chosen regular-hexagon benchmark family, not among all circle placements.","The 108-cell result is a sufficient cellular construction, not a lower bound or an optimum.","The iterative algorithm claims to seek the least number but is randomized and has no global-optimality guarantee.","The paper calls the minimum enclosing circle a circumscribed circle, which can be misleading because it need not pass through all polygon vertices.","Small total site displacement does not prove monotone improvement or convergence of the maximum enclosing-circle radius.","The feasibility step says it counts users, while the implemented continuous model appears to use density times Voronoi area.","Random seeds, independent run counts, success rates, and runtime for the extremal optimizer are not reported.","Selecting the smallest Voronoi area as the least-fit repeater does not directly target the largest radius or largest load violation.","The spanning-tree edge weights are not formally defined even though minimum and maximum trees are central choices.","The claim that minimum-spanning-tree edges do not intersect needs the geometric and graph conditions under which it holds.","For degree k greater than three, deleting k-2 incident edges leaves degree two; if the intended cap is three the count should be k-3.","The component-balance summation leaves the pair index domain and double-counting convention undefined.","A spanning tree changes relay routes but does not by itself enlarge each repeater's user coverage disk, contrary to parts of the explanation.","A node with multiple route neighbors may face incompatible receiver-frequency requirements; direction and duplex constraints are not formalized.","The weather-based reduction from 85.45 to 40 miles is qualitative and unsupported by attenuation data or a revised link budget.","The sentence using R at least Phi as the threshold for any two repeaters to interfere conflicts with the earlier diameter condition R at least 2 Phi.","The eleven-site construction is displayed numerically but the proof does not independently verify every point of the target disk is covered by the listed coordinates.","The optimality proof depends on the exact scope and correctness of cited Theorem r(n) for closed congruent circles.","The 104-site result does not report its maximum enclosing-circle radius, maximum cell load, or an executable constraint audit.","The 90.71 percent Monte Carlo estimate gives no random seed or confidence interval; an approximate 95 percent sampling margin is about 0.18 percentage points.","The 6.4 paragraph refers to a solution in subsection 6.4 itself, likely an erroneous self-reference to subsection 6.2.","Two examples do not establish that adding repeaters generally increases every user's reachable area under the route assignment.","Tables 6 and 8 provide coordinates and channel labels but no machine-checkable proof that every interference and directed-connectivity constraint holds.","Equation 18 gives a parameter-based transition near N=816 with the appendix values, so 'about 1000' is only a coarse description.","Figure 13 lacks independent-run variability, optimum baselines, and uncertainty for the randomized proposed algorithm.","The text calls X Bernoulli, but the displayed count distribution is binomial.","Normalizing each Figure 14 curve by its own maximum hides absolute repeater-count differences.","The two-standard-deviation capacity rule is heuristic and does not specify a target tail probability or simultaneous-overload guarantee.","Approximating the binomial standard deviation by the square root of the mean omits the one-minus-p factor.","A two-meter wavelength alone does not justify a strong general claim that VHF readily bypasses small mountains.","Figure 15 is separated from its caption across a page break and lacks a complete legend.","Multiple lower-tier repeaters sharing one transmit frequency and PL tone can collide on simultaneous uplinks without scheduling or capture assumptions.","Terrain shielding is proposed without path profiles, diffraction calculations, or link-budget validation.","The claim that the method is much faster than simulated annealing is unsupported by runtime tables or experimental settings.","The claim of higher local-optimum escape ability is unsupported by repeated-run success statistics.","The 97.12 percent reachability result is empirical and cannot replace the strong solution's universal reachability condition.","Appendix A leaves the information bit-rate parameter without a numerical value, even though it is needed in the printed capacity derivation.","The symbol table gives V_u and V_u-prime the same description, obscuring their intended difference.","Assumption A5 of no background noise conflicts directly with the use of SNR, N_0, and E_b/N_0.","Assumption A6 about no noisy impact from repeaters is poorly reconciled with I_other, I_self, and the explicit interference condition.","Treating fog, rivers, hills, buildings, and solar activity as one generic interference class mixes propagation loss, obstruction, noise, and fading.","Wikipedia and old web pages support important engineering statements but are unstable or weak primary evidence.","The paper provides no source code, random seeds, machine-readable tables, or complete reproduction package."],"unresolved_resolved":[],"validation_results":[{"details":"41/41 text pages and 41/41 rendered pages passed extraction checks; physical page 1 was recovered visually and complete page-aware visual review is recorded.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_extraction.py"},{"details":"402 language units cover all 17 unique declared protocol chapters with quantitative layouts, reusable slots, variants, and boundaries.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_language_units.py"},{"details":"402/402 units passed with two unrelated original instantiations, no full-clause matches, and no source-fact leaks.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_language_anticopy.py"},{"details":"48 model and algorithm interfaces retain 26 visually reconciled equations plus inputs, conditions, handoffs, validation links, and limitations.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_model_units.py"},{"details":"17 five-part chapter contracts, complete Summary/Introduction paragraph chains, positive organization artifacts, 41 visual dispositions, and 77 last-pass warnings passed.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_learning_audit.py"},{"details":"The paper card, 1011 observations, cumulative B rules, extraction, language/model records, and complete visual review cross-validate.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_paper_artifacts.py"},{"details":"12 nominated B rules received one evidence binding each without lifecycle changes; staged and live canonic