ction but then said never to reach 100 percent for boundary reasons; the denominator and whether launch/exit cells count require a typed definition.","C(Y)=max X(Y)/Y depends on an unstated feasible-set maximum, target distribution, horizon, and crossover policy, so carrying capacity changes meaning across models.","Craft choice is postponed until after scheduling even though some short trips require daily distance beyond the assumed oar maximum.","The guaranteed-distance discussion derives 64 motor miles but later uses 32 oar miles without the same explicit derivation and ignores weather, flow, stops, and within-day congestion.","The campsite-spacing histogram lacks visible sample size, bin specification, source-cleaning procedure, uncertainty, and a direct mapping from the two source rivers to the synthetic river.","Unlimited launch equipment, labor, dock, and arrival capacity can create extreme daily clusters that the objective never penalizes.","Night-only discretization permits arbitrary within-day crossovers and congestion, then treats crossover penalties as a partial repair rather than testing travel feasibility.","The scheduling-theory comparison is qualitative and does not establish that integer programming or alternative network formulations are computationally infeasible.","The naive nearest-available rule does not define tolerance, tie-breaking probability, daily reach boundaries, or rollback after a later night fails.","Priority uses a probability-p sort-or-shuffle step, but no seed, repeated-run protocol, or uncertainty interval is supplied.","Ranking camps primarily by whether they incur a crossover is underspecified when multiple existing trips and order relations are present.","Priority removes trips that cannot be assigned on a night but does not backtrack to reuse space that those trips occupied on earlier nights.","The density model declares all trips equally important but still has to choose signs randomly at identical positions and choose which trip to discard in an unresolved collision.","Equation (1) is explicitly limited to nonzero separation within one campsite distance; exact ties are described by random mapping to plus or minus one outside the displayed formula.","Repeatedly subtracting density has no step size, stop tolerance, maximum iteration count, convergence proof, or test for oscillation and boundary violations.","A one-to-one campsite mapping is asserted for certain near-spacing patterns without a specified matching algorithm or proof for the full set of boats.","Figure 4 reports 1978 trips for Density Perturbation, while the main table reports 1811 for D.Pertb.; scenario or implementation differences are not reconciled.","Bone Heuristic 1 is presented as determining an optimal alternating sequence without proof that other no-crossover launch orders cannot dominate it.","Center squeezing is called a necessary condition but the travel-speed, campsite, launch, and finish constraints used in its feasibility test are not fully formalized.","The greedy score used to select the best inserted trip is not defined mathematically, and no tie rule or complexity analysis is given.","Bone Growth guarantees no crossovers for its constructed schedule but only claims a local optimum and is highly sensitive to the initial bone.","The motor-oar extension is incomplete because the spreading algorithm was not adjusted for the lower flexibility of oar craft.","The final table header is visually ambiguous as 'Var from Distr -10^4'; its scaling must be preserved as uncertain rather than silently read as multiplication or division.","The Density Perturbation row uses Y=200 while the other main comparisons use 45, 150, and 225, preventing a like-for-like ranking.","Bones2 produces more trips largely because it contains shorter trips and substantially misses the stated target distribution, so throughput and distribution fidelity cannot be merged.","Removing high-crossover trips reduced campsite coverage by 10 to 15 percent but no scenario, repeat count, or uncertainty is reported.","Priority crossover growth is described as exponential from three campsite settings without a fitted model, residuals, or broader scale sweep.","The conclusion converts roughly 29 crossovers per trip into less than one contact per hour without defining whether crossover count equals passenger contact or how trip duration enters.","The memo reports carrying capacity 12.5 to 7.5 and omits the approximately 9 trips-per-campsite Bones2 result stated in the body.","The memo says both algorithms can generate any desired trip ratio, while the results show persistent short-trip skew and especially poor Bones2 distribution matching."],"unresolved_resolved":[],"validation_results":[{"details":"The 21/21 rendered pages and 33294 extracted text characters passed extraction checks; physical page 1 has no text layer, remains honestly marked partial-text-review, and its complete Summary was visually transcribed. Complete page-aware visual review is recorded.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_extraction.py"},{"details":"652 language units cover all 14 unique declared protocol chapters with quantitative layouts, reusable slots, variants, and boundaries.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_language_units.py"},{"details":"652/652 units passed with two unrelated original instantiations, no full-clause matches, and no source-fact leaks.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_language_anticopy.py"},{"details":"28 model and algorithm interfaces retain 7 visually reconciled equations plus inputs, conditions, handoffs, validation links, and limitations.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_model_units.py"},{"details":"14 five-part chapter contracts, the exact 2-part Summary/Abstract and 7-paragraph Introduction chains, positive organization artifacts, 10 visual dispositions, and 36 last-pass warnings passed.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_learning_audit.py"},{"details":"The paper card, 1252 observations, cu