ough the model's central claim is interacting positive and negative feedback.","Parameters are assumed unchanged despite phased policies, post-pandemic dynamics, scenario transfer, and claimed nonlinear policy responses.","Reliable tourist, degradation, and satisfaction data are assumed available, but the paper does not release the required data bundle or quality checks.","Several Greek symbols are visually and textually corrupted, and the table does not provide a machine-readable dimension and provenance dictionary.","Equation (1) and Algorithm 1 print an x-squared term multiplied by x/K, producing an unusual cubic crowding term that is not reconciled with the claimed logistic model.","K is set to the 2023 cruise passenger volume rather than an independently estimated environmental or infrastructure carrying capacity.","Equation (3) makes tax rate amplify environmental pressure while tax revenue also funds restoration; the dual mechanism lacks data, units, and causal justification.","Resident satisfaction is treated as dimensionless but Equation (4) and the Euler loop impose no [0,1] or other feasible-state bound.","Equation (5) multiplies a dimensionless tax rate directly by visitor count without a taxable-spending base, so the tax-revenue units are incomplete.","The model requires theta_env+theta_infra+theta_pub=1, but Algorithm 1 inputs omit theta_pub while its satisfaction equation uses it.","The solution says initial conditions are evaluated when T=0 even though T denotes tax revenue; the intended initial time is likely t=0.","Table 2 prints x0 as 165×10^6 while K and the narrative are about 1.65 million visitors, creating a roughly hundredfold scale conflict unless a decimal was lost.","Many parameter rows cite broad reports or topic labels without derivation, units, uncertainty, or a trace from source data to the printed coefficient.","Table 3 lists tau=0.1, yet the policy narrative describes 6.3% as a temporary increase and later sensitivity prose uses 5% or 9% baselines.","Several coefficients are described as weighted combinations of unrelated survey percentages without a published normalization or estimation rule.","L-BFGS-B is said to determine allocation weights, but the objective, bounds, gradients, start point, convergence rule, software version, and result trace are absent.","Algorithm 1 is an explicit-Euler forward simulation, not the named L-BFGS-B optimization that supposedly selects the policy.","Algorithm 1 introduces c_alpha and c_beta without defining or sourcing them in the notation or parameter tables.","The printed satisfaction update contains an unmatched parenthesis and omits a complete executable parameter ledger.","The step size, horizon, numerical stability, stiffness, error tolerance, convergence, and solver comparison are not reported.","The simulation does not constrain degradation, satisfaction, or infrastructure to meaningful ranges and later interprets the absolute value of a possibly negative degradation index as improvement.","Figure 8 has no accompanying numerical table, code, initial-state confirmation, or uncertainty band linking the selected controls to each trajectory.","The prose celebrates a decreasing absolute degradation value, suggesting the state may cross below zero rather than remain a valid nonnegative index.","Resident satisfaction is said to remain high without reported axis values, thresholds, observed comparison, or uncertainty.","Sensitivity is described as based on steady-state results, but the paper does not state the equilibrium solve, burn-in, convergence test, or treatment of multiple equilibria.","Heatmaps omit executable parameter grids, baselines, fixed controls, simulation horizon, convergence, code, and uncertainty.","The claimed nonlinear tax-environment interaction is an internal model output and is not validated against demand or environmental-capacity data.","The reported partial derivative of satisfaction with respect to fixed fees is not derived, tabulated, or reconciled with the model's tax and public-service units.","The claimed 2.3-times environmental-governance efficiency is not accompanied by a formula, baseline, calculation table, or uncertainty.","The 6.3%, 9%, 11-13%, and 13% tax recommendations are not reconciled to one baseline, trigger rule, or feasible implementation path.","The long-term 40% environmental and 45% infrastructure shares leave 15% for public services implicitly, but the allocation and satisfaction consequences are not audited.","The Sanya extension does not publish the promised locally estimated parameters, initial conditions, fit diagnostics, or destination-specific constraints.","The two adverse allocation scenarios are described qualitatively without numerical tables, objectives, feasibility checks, or uncertainty.","The final Sanya allocation exactly repeats Juneau's 0.3/0.4/0.3 policy and is recommended without a local optimization or decision rule.","The under-visited proposal has no spatial demand allocation, substitution, marketing-response, transport-network, or overcrowding-spillover model.","Advantages claim nonlinear feedback and diminishing environmental-investment returns, while the printed equations and assumptions use linear recovery and investment effects.","The authors admit they cannot estimate K and simply use a recent value, undermining the core capacity and visitor-control claims.","The Conclusion says external uncertainties such as climate change were analyzed, while limitations and future work say they were not adequately included.","The Conclusion lists multi-objective optimization as future work even though the Summary and solution already present an optimization claim; the current objective remains unspecified.","The memo first says visitor numbers will always exceed environmental capacity and then says the 6.3% tax and 50-dollar fee can keep them within capacity.","The memo treats 600-700 dollars of additional per-capita expenditure as a satisfaction-enhancing lever without distributional, visitor-welfare, legal, or demand-response analysis.","The reference list and parameter tables do not fully bind each data value, functional form, coefficient, algorithm setting, Sanya input, sensitivity result, and policy claim.","The stated limitations omit the tax-unit problem, initial-state conflict, cubic crowding term, optimizer-simulator mismatch, negative states, policy inconsistencies, and AI provenance.","Recommendations lack responsible owners, legal authority, equity analysis, monitoring data, trigger thresholds, lags, failure conditions, and rollback rules."],"unresolved_resolved":[],"validation_results":[{"details":"All 26 pages retain original extracted text and pixels; pages 2, 5, 6, 14, and 25 remain empty in the immutable text extraction and are recovered through separately hashed OCR and bounded visual review. The frozen review binds 19 printed or algorithm formula records, 14 figures, three tables, eight other structures, seven OCR artifacts, and nine JPEG proxies across two context segments.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_extraction.py"},{"details":"All physical pages were read in order. Positive coverage retains Summary, Introduction, model preparation, architecture, assumptions, notation, coupled equations, Juneau solution, sensitivity and phased policy, Sanya and under-visited transfer, evaluation, conclusion, and memo; Contents, reference entries, AI-use report, commercial watermark, QR material, and running furniture remain excluded from positive human-language evidence.","status":"pass","validator":"source_coverage_audit"},{"details":"713 source-anchored language units cover 13 normalized chapters and preserve concrete place-pressure openings, task-method-result ladders, data-triggered model choice, premise-consequence assumptions, mechanism-before-equation prose, explicit policy-choice verbs, visual-first readings, failed-scenario comparison, phased recommendations, local-bottleneck transfer, candid 