 that produces them.","The particulate-health caveat discusses relocation to low-density areas without a spatial dispersion or population-exposure model.","Emissions use a present-day generation mix over a 50-year horizon, while the discussion separately assumes coal's share will decline.","Table 3.2 reports GHGs increasing 24% while the CO2 scenario plot falls; the species, baseline, and accounting distinction are not reconciled.","Figure 3.6 provides internal trajectories without frozen numeric series, uncertainty bands, or an observed benchmark.","The oil-consumption curves are not accompanied by a visible formula, per-vehicle consumption model, or data table.","A 2060 charging demand is compared with 2009 annual electricity consumption without generation growth, peak-load timing, or capacity constraints.","Figure 3.8's caption says several billion MWh, while the plotted and stated maximum is about 1.04 billion MWh.","The Results section says the grid appears capable of meeting added demand, while the Discussion says demand could be unmanageable.","The paper acknowledges region-specific competition parameters but applies one aggregate US parameter set without regional calibration.","The $103-per-vehicle health estimate is based on tailpipe particulate emissions and is not re-estimated for relocated power-plant exposure.","Future dollar differentials rely on source-era fuel, resale, maintenance, and technology assumptions without propagated uncertainty.","The economic and growth models are described as related, but the cost differential is not quantitatively mapped into competition coefficients.","The conclusion calls model behavior realistic despite no calibration, held-out validation, benchmark, or completed growth-model sensitivity analysis.","The 53% carbon reduction, 70% oil reduction, and several-million-job headlines are not tied to one reproducible scenario trace.","The conclusion says subsidies are currently needed, but the body does not present a clearly