-C-VER-002","R-C-VER-003","R-C-RESULT-001","R-C-DEC-001","R-C-ECO-001","R-C-CAUS-001","R-C-VIZ-001","R-C-LANG-001","R-C-AI-001"]},"unresolved_added":["The Summary's national-crisis framing does not itself establish model validity.","The official problem statement is not reproduced; task reconstruction is paper-internal.","NFLIS drug reports are treated as representative of overall drug use without measurement-error analysis.","Five eastern states omit neighboring-state influence at borders.","The 2010-2017 observation window is short for epidemic waves and long-horizon prediction.","Census data end in 2016 while NFLIS data extend to 2017; temporal alignment is incomplete.","Removing never-measured, partial-year, and incomplete factors may introduce selection bias.","The data-cleaning rules lack a machine-readable factor dictionary and deletion log.","County-centre coordinates are not representative for unusually shaped counties.","County area and FIPS/geography provenance are external inputs not frozen in the paper.","Reports-per-area is only a proxy for drug-use density and can reflect policing/reporting intensity.","The gravity analogy is not a causal mechanism for human drug spread.","Inverse-square distance is selected without comparing exponential or network alternatives.","The Haversine formula's radians, Earth radius, and coordinate units are not fully audited.","External influence sums over the selected counties and omits out-of-sample neighboring states.","The assumption that rural populations are more susceptible to outside influence is untested.","The assumption that no new drug appears through 2025 conflicts with the paper's epidemic-wave background.","The assumption that addiction continues its current trend without external influence is strong and unvalidated.","Quadratic weighting `(year-2009)^2` needs sign/orientation and normalization clarification.","Recent-year emphasis can overreact to reporting shocks or one-off outbreaks.","The trend derivative requires at least two years of reports, leaving sparse counties weakly represented.","The normalization ratio median/current influence can be unstable near zero or extreme values.","The spread update adds external influence and trend without a calibrated time or count scale.","The MATLAB iteration is implementation evidence, not independently executed verification.","Drug-specific extensions face low counts and too few reporting counties.","One-step validation compares 2014-based predictions with 2015, but the split and all preprocessing are not frozen.","The slope-one reference line is useful visually but does not measure calibration across count ranges.","Least-squares slope and residual sum are sensitive to high-count counties.","The 0.9276 versus 0.9262 slope comparison is a marginal improvement and should not be called strong validation.","The 1.9% and 3.94% improvements use unclear denominator conventions.","Four-step validation compounds model errors and still compares against a baseline with different information content.","The claim of beating baseline at every step is based on the displayed sample, not a confidence interval.","Appendix baseline figures are not an independent validation dataset.","High-influence counties are said to be less affected by outside influence, but the causal direction is assumed by the normalization.","The claim that synthetics spread faster than natural opiates is not separated from potency, reporting, or availability effects.","The claim of 400-6000 times morphine strength comes from an external source and needs exact opioid mapping.","The origin score `G_{d,i}` depends on the same influence proxy used for the main model.","Maximum origin score does not prove geographic origin or transmission direction.","The 3-Methylfentanyl map is contextual corroboration, not causal validation.","The 90th percentile threshold is a policy choice with no loss or intervention calibration.","County-specific scaling by influence does not guarantee equal public-health risk.","Eleven counties are already above threshold in 2017, but the source's lists and summary counts need exact reconciliation.","Six additional counties cross by 2025 under a no-intervention assumption; this is conditional, not inevitable.","No county falls below threshold because the model excludes effective intervention before strategy simulation.","Median R-squared ranks association, not causation or predictive value.","R-squared distributions can hide sign, lag, nonlinear, and county-level heterogeneity.","The three selected Census factors are chosen in-sample and may overfit the available counties.","The factor with the highest median R-squared is used alone without cross-validation or model selection uncertainty.","The polynomial adjustment equation is described as multivariate but its degree and estimation details are incomplete.","Socio-economic derivatives are extrapolated outside observed data for future trend updates.","Adjusted slope .9288 is only slightly above .9276, while residual reduction is larger; metric disagreement needs explanation.","The 12.2% residual reduction may be driven by scale or high-count counties.","Strategy explanations for associate degrees and veteran status are plausible narratives, not identified mechanisms.","Education, job, and recovery programs have no cost, capacity, uptake, or causal effect model.","Evidence from Chinese needle exchange and methadone programs may not transfer to U.S. counties without context matching.","The recovery-center plot is a modeled slope adjustment, not an observed intervention result.","Doubling 2015 reports is an artificial shock and only tests one scale path.","Doubling 2014 and 2015 reports tests level changes but not reporting-process changes.","The report-scale sensitivity conclusion is local and cannot establish global robustness.","Doubling the leading Census factor creates a large residual but is not a policy-realistic perturbation by itself.","Socio-economic sensitivity is tested for one factor and one year, not a joint parameter region.","The conclusion broadens county-level conditional results to national epidemic claims.","The model's “easy verification” depends on unarchived input files and preprocessing decisions.","Neighboring states, urban/rural heterogeneity, policing intensity, treatment access, and mortality are not jointly modeled.","Yearly time steps cannot resolve rapid outbreaks or intervention timing.","New opioid waves are explicitly omitted and bias is expected upward, but the magnitude is unknown.","Weaknesses identify omitted factors but do not provide a completed repair or uncertainty interval.","The DEA memo compresses body findings and rounds no exact metrics; it should remain traceable to body tables.","The memo says six counties pass the threshold while the body lists eleven initially above and six additional; the denominator distinction must stay explicit.","The memo's Ohio mortality statement is not derived from the model evidence presented in the body.","The memo recommends programs while omitting monitoring, authority, budget, equity, and exit criteria.","References and external maps are evidence sources, not author positive-language units.","Appendix baseline plots are visual evidence only and must not inflate language coverage.","Formula glyphs and OCR symbols such as delta, phi, and summation need bounded visual recovery.","The paper contains typographical slips (`when then`, `should must`, duplicated articles) that should be observed as human texture, not copied as style defaults."],"unresolved_resolved":[],"validation_results":[{"details":"All 25 physical pages retain page records and 200-DPI rasters; Summary, Introduction, cleaning/assumptions, 12 formula structures, ten labeled figures, three numbered tables, validation baselines, origin/threshold, socioeconomic adjustment, policy/sensitivity, strengths/weaknesses, DEA memo, References and appendix boundary are bound.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_extraction.py"},{"details":"Pages 1-25 were read in physical order; Contents, formula/table/caption regions, References and appendix plot labels remain excluded from positive prose, while the Summary, literal Introduction, analytical body, conclusion, DEA memo and explicit weaknesses remain author prose.","status":"pass","validator":"source_coverage_audit"},{"details":"The audit retains data-cleaning motives, proxy caveats, baseline-before-validation, modest one-step gain, multi-step degradation, drug-specific origin, explicit 90th-percentile trigger, median-R-squared factor screening, socioeconomic adjustment, association-bounded policy language, sensitivity limits, explicit repair directions and DEA memo compression.","status":"pass","validator":"human_style_learning_audit"},{"details":"243 source-anchored language units cover eight normalized chapters with occurrences, function, order, slots, variants, boundary, disposition and anti-copy restrictions.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_language_units.py"},{"details":"All 243 reviewed author-prose units passed with two unrelated instantiations, zero source-fact leaks, zero full-clause matches and maximum shared content run two; Contents, References and appendix labels are absent.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_language_anticopy.py"},{"details":"Eight chapter contracts preserve inputs, rhetorical jobs, analytical jobs, outputs and handoffs while excluding watermark, form furniture, Contents, formulas, tables, captions, chart labels, References and code from positive prose.","status":"pass","validator":"organization_logic_audit"},{"details":"Nine typed model units retain twelve formula structures and nine dependency handoffs; data construction, local spread, outside influence, prediction, bounded validation, origin/trigger, covariate screen, adjusted prediction and policy handoff remain separated.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_model_units.py"},{"details":"Language 243 is stric