g relationships similarly despite size, shape, highways, and distance.","The Markov assumption omits policy and long-term memory that the limitations later admit.","The random walk's stochastic process is not fully specified by a reproducible seed, trial count, or code release.","Four class thresholds are selected from histograms and asymmetric standard-deviation rules without calibration.","The crisis-period sample is already abnormal, so its mean and standard deviation are not a stable general baseline.","The six-class revision increases flexibility after model development and requires multiplicity/selection control.","Assigning no-data counties low stable can create artificial spatial stability.","The visual ring/origin discovery is explicitly not formally quantified.","Human pattern recognition is cited as support, but visual resemblance is not spatial inference.","Inverse-distance tallies use 1, 1/4, and implicit distance units without an estimated decay law.","The penalty matrix encodes author expectations about transitions without a learning or elicitation protocol.","The penalty matrix initially over-clusters the model, proving the transition costs can dominate results.","The Moran-inspired error measures class neighbor agreement, not the original prevalence or rate error.","The printed Equation 2 absolute-value convention is visually ambiguous and must be bound to the raster.","Optimizing noise and class coefficients against 2010-2015 risks fitting the same spatial patterns later evaluated.","Noise level 0.3 is selected without a confidence interval or seed sensitivity region.","Later improvement from noise compounding may reflect distribution matching rather than county-level accuracy.","Three-month interpolation between annual ACS values is convenient but may create artificial transitions.","The time-step choice is partly made because coefficients can fit it and annual comparison is easier.","Random-forest test accuracy 90% is not enough without class balance, confusion metrics, calibration, or held-out design details.","Feature importance ranks magnitude and gives no direction of influence.","The 23 socioeconomic predictors and later 54 predictors are selected through different screens, complicating comparison.","Optimized socioeconomic and non-socioeconomic random walks become comparable, so the added variables do not establish a useful branch.","The overfit argument relies on a falling 2016 error despite no independent future test data.","Time pressure causes coefficient precision to be reduced; the resulting error is acknowledged but not quantified by precision level.","The origin backcast uses the old model to avoid SES extrapolation, but that changes the model mechanism used for origin inference.","Initializing every 2000 county low stable lets random noise create unrealistic one-step high states.","The visual origin is near the model origin, not proof that the model found the actual origin.","SAR, SEM, and SDM distinguish spillover and correlated risk conceptually, but observational data cannot identify the mechanisms alone.","Spatial weights from a shapefile omit major roads, population flows, and distance variation.","Missing counties in the shapefile/ACS alignment create selection and matching risks.","Numerical convergence trouble may indicate scaling, collinearity, or model misspecification; successful convergence does not select among them.","VIF>10 removal is heuristic and the VIF<4 sensitivity is not a full variable-selection uncertainty analysis.","Keeping VIF 8-9 education variables for literature reasons can preserve multicollinearity and policy confounding.","Separate yearly regressions are forced by package limits and are not equivalent to a panel model.","Aggregating seven annual fits obscures temporal dependence and multiple testing.","Significant LM/BP tests leave omitted spatial variables and heteroskedasticity unresolved.","Asymptotic spatial tests may be inaccurate for the county/predictor ratio, and the needed Monte Carlo tests were not run.","Highest Durbin log likelihood does not prove the Durbin mechanism or causal superiority.","Direct, indirect, and total impacts depend on W, model specification, and feedback assumptions.","The total illicit drug rate includes opioid use, creating part-whole and endogeneity concerns.","The 52% total impact is treated as realistic through CDC proportions, but external plausibility is not identification.","Ordinary regression's 37.4% coefficient and the spatial direct impact 37.2% do not alone prove indirect diffusion.","AIC and likelihood comparisons require comparable likelihood assumptions and do not establish public-health effect validity.","Ancestry, language, and family variables can reflect structural inequality rather than modifiable causes.","Predictors deemed unhelpful for policy may still matter for confounding control or descriptive inference.","The small-subset sensitivity result being similar does not prove variable-level robustness.","`Mostly` positive/negative is a 5:2 majority rule across seven years and ignores magnitude and uncertainty.","Linear SES forecast inputs become less accurate with horizon, so the spatial forecast is conditional and fragile.","Comparing both forecasts through the six-stage classifier can hide continuous-rate differences.","The random walk's medium-horizon error balance does not guarantee future performance.","The problematic-county rule has a unit inconsistency: one and a half years is not 24 quarterly steps.","The forecasted regional decline may be displacement outside the five states rather than improvement.","Total illicit drug reduction is a broad upstream lever, but the model does not identify how to cause the rate reduction.","The proposed 0.1% reduction and 0.052% opioid reduction inherit observational impact assumptions.","Prescription restrictions can harm patients with chronic/severe pain if access safeguards fail.","Public education, PDMPs, take-back, naloxone, MAT, and diversion require capacity, budget, uptake, legal authority, and monitoring.","A second war on drugs is rejected on historical grounds, but the replacement policy bundle is not evaluated experimentally.","Criminal-justice statistics and treatment proposals carry distributional and stigma risks.","MAUP means county-level findings can change at census-tract, ZIP, or individual scale.","Adjacency versus distance is explicitly unresolved and could significantly alter both models.","Markov long-term memory and policy feedback are omitted from the random walk.","Linear interpolation/extrapolation of socioeconomic percentages can violate bounds or compositional constraints.","The spatial forecast applies the 2016 model despite 2016 not necessarily representing 2010-2016.","No future 2017-2021 observations are used to validate the forecast curves.","The memo says the epidemic will continue across the nation while the study only models five states.","References, Contents, captions, map labels, table cells, algorithm furniture, and appendix predictor rows must not inflate positive human-language counts."],"unresolved_resolved":[],"validation_results":[{"details":"All 29 physical pages retain page records and 200-DPI rasters; Summary, Introduction, drug taxonomy, six-state random walk, clustering repair, socioeconomic augmentation, 12 formula structures, 12 numbered figures, spatial-regression diagnostics, impacts, sensitivity, forecast horizons, limitations, future work, DEA memo, References, and appendix boundary are bound.","status":"pass","validator":"validate_extraction.py"},{"details":"Pages 1-29 were read in physical order; title, Contents, equations, algorithm furniture, table cells, captions, map labels, References, and appendix predictor rows remain excluded from positive prose, while Summary, literal Introduction, analytical body, strategies, conclusion, future exploration, and DEA memo remain author prose.","status":"pass","validator":"source_coverage_a