G-002","R-C-LOG-003","R-C-ASSUM-001","R-C-DATA-001","R-C-DATA-002","R-C-DATA-003","R-C-MOD-001","R-C-MOD-002","R-C-MOD-003","R-C-MOD-004","R-C-MOD-005","R-C-MOD-006","R-C-MOD-007","R-C-VER-001","R-C-VER-002","R-C-VER-003","R-C-RESULT-001","R-C-DEC-001","R-C-ECO-001","R-C-CAUS-001","R-C-VIZ-001","R-C-LANG-001","R-C-AI-001"]},"unresolved_added":["NFLIS drug-identification counts are laboratory/enforcement observations, not direct prevalence, addiction, or person counts.","The paper shifts among opioid, synthetic opioid, heroin, all substances, drug reports, and opioid use without a stable outcome dictionary.","Filling a missing total by adjacent-year interpolation does not recover the missing drug identity.","The rule for more than three missing years discards counties without a coverage sensitivity analysis.","The source and quality of the NFLIS missingness process are not audited.","Counties absent from NFLIS are declared insignificant without testing coverage bias.","The Haversine radius/unit convention is not fully bound to a distance threshold because no threshold is estimated.","Heat-map concentration near transport hubs, lakes, and coasts is descriptive and not measured transport flow.","Assumption 1 treats surrounding counties and historical data as sufficient determinants without a spillover or lag test.","Assumption 2 freezes government intervention through 2026 and makes the forecast conditional on no excessive rectification.","Assumption 3 calls incomplete data realistic enough without an error model.","Assumption 4 removes non-NFLIS counties and can truncate the spread boundary.","CA cell status c=0/1 omits partial county representation and boundary uncertainty.","Sorting neighbors before Euclidean distance removes identity and assumes exchangeability of ordered neighbors.","The nearest-k environment depends strongly on k and coordinate quality.","The environmental vector mixes growth and counts without documented scaling.","Euclidean distance on raw report counts is dominated by large-count counties.","The KNN history match has no out-of-sample split or no-change comparator.","The inverse-distance weight has no calibrated exponent and no explicit zero-distance convention.","The Figure 3 worked example demonstrates mechanics but not predictive accuracy.","Growth rates can be negative, yet the bounds and treatment of negative forecasts are not specified.","The CA update uses historical environments as if they remain comparable in future years.","CI is a concentration measure and cannot by itself identify diffusion or transmission.","State aggregation can hide county-level reversals and population differences.","The interpretation that smaller CI means stronger diffusion depends on the chosen share denominator.","Claims that aggregation points disappear because Ohio is cracking down are not supported by enforcement data.","Claims about Lexington-centered heroin spread are map interpretations without origin validation.","The 2017-2026 forecast horizon is long relative to the admitted short-term validation strength.","The Ohio threshold 120,000 is author-selected from a plot rather than an independently justified public-health limit.","Crossing a threshold in 2026 is a model trigger, not proof that government concern will occur.","RMSE averages county errors and can overweight populous/reporting-heavy counties.","The annual and integrated RMSE formulas do not state whether missing/discarded counties share the denominator.","k=12,m=2 is selected from one RMSE heat map without a stability region or nested validation.","Appendix I contains many parameter combinations but no seed, runtime, or reproducibility manifest.","Socioeconomic estimate and percent fields may carry survey and denominator error that is not propagated.","The 2010-2012 and 2013-2016 attribute-vintage discrepancy is acknowledged but not harmonized.","The stated split into 2010-2011 and 2012-2017 is inconsistent with the preceding 2010-2012/2013-2016 table.","Removing '(X)' margin-of-error fields may create informative missingness.","Dropping estimate-only values assumes percentage structure is the relevant signal without testing alternatives.","Extreme first/last quartiles are selected after observing the report distribution and can exaggerate contrasts.","The homogeneity test and ANOVA/correlation branch lacks multiple-testing correction across hundreds of attributes.","ANOVA on extreme groups and correlation on all counties use different samples, making their significance incomparable.","The paper calls the sample mismatch localization but does not quantify localized effects.","Selected marital, education, ancestry, and language factors may proxy structural inequality rather than modifiable causes.","Reciprocal transforms amplify small percentages and can create unstable outliers.","The ancestry reciprocal sum combines six populations without a principled weighting or collinearity check.","The language reciprocal transform is sensitive to small denominators and coding conventions.","Adding transformed factors to the environment changes distance geometry and invalidates direct comparison with the original model.","The modified environment does not document scaling between four transformed variables and opioid counts.","Positive association for never-married status is converted to a responsibility/entertainment story without individual-level evidence.","Positive association for higher education is converted to pressure and opioid-seeking without causal identification.","Ancestry explanations risk stigmatizing mixed populations and implying trafficking without measurements.","Language explanations risk stigmatizing non-native speakers and treating communication difficulty as addiction mechanism.","The original/modified map comparison has no quantitative map agreement or uncertainty measure.","Virginia concentration changes may be driven by transformation scale or reporting volume rather than factor effects.","The modified model's k=5,m=2 minimum differs from the original k=12,m=2, but no paired validation design explains the selection.","The reported modified RMSE 98.70 is not accompanied by confidence intervals or a baseline error.","Strategy principles assume one action changes one variable directly and ignore interactions among socioeconomic factors.","Strategy One changes marital status through tax/mortgage discounts without estimating uptake, legality, cost, or equity.","Strategy Two changes English proficiency through a training institution without modeling enrollment, completion, or access.","Table 5 treats simulated values below actual 2017 reports as action effectiveness even though the simulation error is not separated from intervention effect.","Table 5's 5% overshoot is attributed to error without a calibrated error distribution.","Table 6 has sign/label inconsistencies between text and table headings for the language change rates.","The conclusion combines two model-conditional reductions into an unconditional claim that both actions effectively reduce cases.","Strength claims about innovation do not establish superiority over CA, KNN, regression, or spatial baselines.","The long-term weakness admits large forecast error but the memo still reports 2026 threshold certainty.","The overfit concern is stated because validation data are small, but no holdout or regularization remedy is supplied.","Excluding counties with no original abuse data prevents the model from representing first emergence in unseen counties.","The memo repeats map-derived transport and spread claims without adding uncertainty or evidence qualifiers.","The Appendix RMSE grid and all table/figure cells must remain model/visual evidence rather than positive human-language counts."],"unresolved_resolved":[],"validation_results":[{"details":"All 23 physical pages retain text and 200-DPI rasters; Summary, memo, Introduction, CA/KNN, CI, RMSE, factor screening, modified model, strategy tables, weaknesse